WEBVTT

legitimacy-06-1
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:06.350
Previously: both repairs failed—the
picture to be repaired was already gone

legitimacy-06-2
00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:07.360
from the frame.

legitimacy-06-3
00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:13.940
This time: the frame is empty, but a new
group arrives onstage.

legitimacy-06-4
00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:20.470
Episode 6: Dissenters Resemble a Small
State

legitimacy-06-5
00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:29.430
Imagine a national theater: a big stage,
a big flag, and an audience full of “us.”

legitimacy-06-6
00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:33.380
As earlier episodes explained, this
play's meaning has dispersed. The

legitimacy-06-7
00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:37.560
audience leaves one by one. The big flag
still hangs, but no one watches.

legitimacy-06-8
00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:44.660
Yet the desire for a “we,” for someone to
represent us, has not dispersed with it.

legitimacy-06-9
00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:46.867
Author's thesis, chapter 4 introduction:
after the symbolic collapse of the

legitimacy-06-10
00:00:46.867 --> 00:00:48.928
nation–state–sovereignty triad, the
desire for collective identity and

legitimacy-06-11
00:00:48.928 --> 00:00:50.370
representational legitimacy remains.
(Paraphrase)

legitimacy-06-12
00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:57.894
Then a group brings little flags and a
little lectern of its own, and starts

legitimacy-06-13
00:00:57.894 --> 00:01:00.750
performing again in front of the empty
stage.

legitimacy-06-14
00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:05.860
They do not mount the state's high
platform. They connect horizontally below

legitimacy-06-15
00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:10.567
it, gathering through shared
marginalization, emotional bonds, and the

legitimacy-06-16
00:01:10.567 --> 00:01:12.450
very fact of being excluded.

legitimacy-06-17
00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:17.245
Transversal dissent: authority comes from
shared marginalization, emotional

legitimacy-06-18
00:01:17.245 --> 00:01:22.169
solidarity, and the visibility of “being
excluded,” rather than institutional

legitimacy-06-19
00:01:22.169 --> 00:01:23.000
embeddedness.

legitimacy-06-20
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:26.020
Author's thesis §4.1, drawing on
Bleiker's (2000) concept of “transversal

legitimacy-06-21
00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:26.930
dissent.” (Paraphrase)

legitimacy-06-22
00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:39.430
Pause: compare the new stage with the
old. Can you find three similarities?

legitimacy-06-23
00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:45.059
Flags, a lectern, and a line separating
“us” from “them.” The stage is smaller;

legitimacy-06-24
00:01:45.059 --> 00:01:46.840
the grammar is unchanged.

legitimacy-06-25
00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:48.989
Author's thesis §4.1: these groups differ
organizationally from the state, yet

legitimacy-06-26
00:01:48.989 --> 00:01:51.165
their discursive strategies often imitate
the nation-state, building a symbolic

legitimacy-06-27
00:01:51.165 --> 00:01:53.342
“we” through collective suffering, mythic
memory, or exclusionary binaries (the

legitimacy-06-28
00:01:53.342 --> 00:01:55.160
thesis cites Brown, 2001; Agathangelou &amp;
Ling, 2004). (Paraphrase)

legitimacy-06-29
00:01:56.460 --> 00:02:03.810
The author calls this a Transient
Legitimacy Polity.

legitimacy-06-30
00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:09.076
Its legitimacy comes neither from stable
institutions nor from a coherent history,

legitimacy-06-31
00:02:09.076 --> 00:02:13.571
but from its performative identification
with “the oppressed” and “the

legitimacy-06-32
00:02:13.571 --> 00:02:14.470
marginalized.”

legitimacy-06-33
00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:20.020
It depends on the urgency of “speaking
out now,” rather than a vision of “what

legitimacy-06-34
00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:23.720
comes next.” In a phrase: legitimacy
without legacy.

legitimacy-06-35
00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:26.686
Author's thesis §4.1: “It is legitimacy
without legacy.” Its legitimacy comes

legitimacy-06-36
00:02:26.686 --> 00:02:28.920
from “the urgency of voice, not the
durability of vision.”

legitimacy-06-37
00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:34.660
Why does the new stage look like the old
one?

legitimacy-06-38
00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:40.181
Because resistance rarely stands outside
power. It often grows within it,

legitimacy-06-39
00:02:40.181 --> 00:02:44.870
rewriting the old ways of speaking rather
than replacing them.

legitimacy-06-40
00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:47.182
Author's thesis §4.1, citing Butler
(1997): resistance rarely lies outside

legitimacy-06-41
00:02:47.182 --> 00:02:49.618
the power structures it challenges. It is
often embedded in them, resignifying

legitimacy-06-42
00:02:49.618 --> 00:02:51.680
dominant discourse rather than directly
replacing it. (Paraphrase)

legitimacy-06-43
00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:56.797
A caveat: a Transient Legitimacy Polity
is a symptom, not an accusation. The

legitimacy-06-44
00:02:56.797 --> 00:02:59.910
suffering of those onstage is real, and
so is their exclusion.

legitimacy-06-45
00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:05.316
It is neither pure liberation nor
complete co-optation. It shows only that,

legitimacy-06-46
00:03:05.316 --> 00:03:09.280
beyond the old language, we have no other
language yet.

legitimacy-06-47
00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:11.688
Author's thesis §4.1: a Transient
Legitimacy Polity is not just a new

legitimacy-06-48
00:03:11.688 --> 00:03:14.480
political entity but a symptom—“neither
purely emancipatory nor fully co-opted.”

legitimacy-06-49
00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:18.420
This episode's reference card (take a
screenshot)

legitimacy-06-50
